\title{The Three-Strata Plane:\\ A Measured-Exponent Calculus for the Cost of Integer Factoring}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We introduce a single coordinate---the \emph{measured exponent}
$\alpha(f)=\lim_{x\to\infty}\log f(x)/x$ of a cost profile $f$ expressed in the
bit-size variable $x=\log N$---and use it to place three qualitatively different
approaches to factoring a semiprime $N=pq$ on one plane. We develop the calculus
of this exponent: uniqueness, invariance under $O(1)$ additive corrections to
$\log f$, two calibration points ($e^{ax}$ has exponent $a$; every polynomial in
$x$ has exponent $0$), a \emph{units lemma} stating that re-expressing a profile
in a rescaled variable $x=cb$ divides its exponent by $c$, and a \emph{price
theorem} stating that two profiles with exponents $a<b$ are separated by a ratio
whose own exponent is exactly $b-a$ and which therefore diverges. Against this
coordinate we locate three strata: the \emph{definition-routes}
($\tau$, $\sigma_1$ evaluated from $N$ alone) at $\alpha=1/2$, with an exact
$O(1)$ closed-form inversion and a hitting-set lower bound
$|S|\ge\pi(B)-1$ on candidate lists; the \emph{classical methods} (trial
division, Fermat, Pollard $\rho$), each given an exact statement, at
$\alpha=1/4$; and the \emph{quantum corner}, whose classical split step is
unconditionally exact, at $\alpha=0$. The three exponents $0<1/4<1/2$ are
distinct, the profiles eventually strictly ordered, and the quantum stratum is
polynomially bounded while the two classical strata provably are not. The gap
between the first two is realised as the \emph{price of structure-blindness}
$N^{1/4}$: strictly increasing, unbounded, and---carrying the same exponent as
the method it replaces---a unit exchange rate between ignorance and work.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} integer factoring; measured exponent; semiprime;
divisor sum; Pollard rho; Fermat factorization; Shor's algorithm; hitting-set
lower bound.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem with ``how hard is factoring?''}

Statements about the difficulty of factoring an integer come from at least three
incommensurable traditions. Analytic number theory speaks of arithmetic
functions whose values encode the factorization. Algorithmics speaks of trial
division, Fermat's method, Pollard $\rho$, and the sieves. Quantum computation
speaks of period-finding. Each reports costs in its own units---divisions, gcds,
gate counts---measured against its own size parameter: the integer, its square
root, the bit-length, or the bit-length of a prime factor.

The consequence is that the folklore ordering of these approaches, though
broadly correct, is not \emph{checkable}. Two cost statements cannot be compared
unless they live on the same coordinate; and if they cannot be compared, a unit
error in one of them is not a contradiction but an inconsistency nobody is
positioned to notice.

\subsection{The remedy: one coordinate, measured not asserted}

Fix the bit-size variable $x=\log N$ and, for a cost profile
$f:\RR\to\RR$, define
\[
  \alpha(f) \;=\; \lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{\log f(x)}{x}.
\]
A profile behaving like $N^{\alpha}$ returns $\alpha$; a profile polynomial in
the bit-size returns $0$. The invariant is coarse on purpose: it discards
constants, logarithmic factors and hardware---the quantities that differ between
traditions and between decades---and retains the shape of the growth. It is also
exactly the quantity an empirical log--log fit estimates, so theory and
measurement speak the same language.

Throughout, $N=pq$ denotes a semiprime with $p,q$ distinct primes, and unless
stated $p<q$. Logarithms in $\alpha$ are natural; empirical tables use
$\log_2$, which changes no exponent.

\section{The measured-exponent calculus}

\subsection{Definition and uniqueness}

\begin{definition}[Measured exponent]
A profile $f:\RR\to\RR$ \emph{has measured exponent} $a$, written
$\alpha(f)=a$, if $\log f(x)/x \to a$ as $x\to\infty$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness]\label{thm:unique}
A profile has at most one measured exponent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Limits in a Hausdorff space are unique.
\end{proof}

Trivial as it is, Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} converts a disagreement about
exponents into a genuine contradiction; it is the engine of the units ledger in
Section~\ref{sec:units}.

\subsection{Robustness}

\begin{theorem}[Invariance under bounded log-correction]\label{thm:robust}
If there is a constant $C$ with $|\log f(x)-ax|\le C$ for all sufficiently large
$x$, then $\alpha(f)=a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $\log f(x)/x = a + (\log f(x)-ax)/x$. The second term is bounded by
$C/x\to0$; squeeze.
\end{proof}

This is the formal content of ``constants do not matter'': multiplying a cost by
a fixed factor, or adding a fixed amount of preprocessing, perturbs $\log f$ by
$O(1)$ and leaves $\alpha$ fixed.

\subsection{Calibration points}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential calibration]\label{thm:expcal}
For all $a,c$, $\alpha\bigl(x\mapsto e^{ax+c}\bigr)=a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\log e^{ax+c}-ax=c$ is bounded; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:robust}.
\end{proof}

Since $x=\log N$, the profile $e^{ax}$ \emph{is} $N^{a}$.

\begin{theorem}[Polynomial calibration]\label{thm:polycal}
For $C>0$ and any real $d$, $\alpha\bigl(x\mapsto Cx^{d}\bigr)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $x>0$, $\log(Cx^{d})/x = \log C/x + d(\log x/x)$, and both terms vanish,
the second because $\log x = o(x)$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:polycal} identifies ``polynomial in the number of digits''---the
standard notion of tractability---with the single point $\alpha=0$.

\subsection{The units lemma}

Empirical work rarely fits against $x=\log N$ directly: for methods governed by
the smaller prime, the natural regressor is $b=\log p$, and for a balanced
semiprime $\log N = 2\log p$.

\begin{theorem}[Units lemma]\label{thm:units}
If $\alpha(f)=s$ and $c>0$, then $\alpha\bigl(x\mapsto f(x/c)\bigr)=s/c$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
As $x\to\infty$ so does $x/c$, hence $\log f(x/c)\big/(x/c)\to s$; dividing by
the constant $c$ gives $\log f(x/c)/x \to s/c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:units}
A slope of $s$ per prime-bit corresponds, for a balanced semiprime, to an
exponent $s/2$ on $N$. Reading a per-prime-bit slope directly as an exponent on
$N$ overstates it by a factor of two.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Positive exponent forces superpolynomial growth}

\begin{theorem}[Eventual exponential lower bound]\label{thm:superpoly}
If $\alpha(f)=a>0$ and $f$ is eventually positive, then $e^{(a/2)x}\le f(x)$ for
all sufficiently large $x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Eventually $\log f(x)/x>a/2$, i.e.\ $\log f(x)>(a/2)x$; exponentiate, using
$f(x)=e^{\log f(x)}$ where $f(x)>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:superpoly}
Under the same hypotheses $f(x)\to\infty$, $f$ is superpolynomial, and $f$ is
not polynomially bounded.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The price theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Ratio exponent]\label{thm:ratio}
If $f,g$ are eventually positive with $\alpha(f)=a$, $\alpha(g)=b$, then
$\alpha(g/f)=b-a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\log(g(x)/f(x))=\log g(x)-\log f(x)$ where both are positive; divide by $x$ and
subtract the limits.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Price theorem]\label{thm:price}
If in addition $a<b$, then $\alpha(g/f)=b-a>0$ and $g(x)/f(x)\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:ratio} with Corollary~\ref{cor:superpoly}.
\end{proof}

A separation in exponent is therefore never a constant overhead; it is an
unbounded penalty with a measurable exponent of its own.

\section{Stratum A: the definition-routes}

A \emph{definition-route} reads a witness off from $N$ alone by evaluating a
classical arithmetic function, then inverts it. No structural feature of $N$ is
exploited beyond divisibility testing.

\subsection{The witnesses are exact}

\begin{theorem}[Divisors of a semiprime]\label{thm:divisors}
For distinct primes $p,q$, the divisor set of $pq$ is $\{1,p,q,pq\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The divisors of a product of coprimes are products of divisors of the factors;
the four resulting products are distinct because $p\neq q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact values]\label{thm:exact}
For distinct primes $p,q$ and $N=pq$,
\[
  \tau(N)=4,\qquad \sigma_1(N)=1+p+q+pq=1+p+q+N .
\]
\end{theorem}

Both identities hold exactly at every size, with no error term---this is what
distinguishes a definition-route from a heuristic.

\begin{corollary}[The oracle hands over the factor sum]
$\sigma_1(N)-N-1=p+q$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Inversion is $O(1)$}

\begin{theorem}[Vieta uniqueness]\label{thm:vieta}
If $a+b=c+d$ and $ab=cd$ then $\{a,b\}=\{c,d\}$; ordering by $a\le b$, $c\le d$
forces $a=c$, $b=d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both pairs are the root multiset of $X^2-sX+m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact discriminant]\label{thm:disc}
For $p\le q$, $(p+q)^2-4pq=(q-p)^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Closed-form $\sigma_1$-route]\label{thm:closedform}
Let $N=pq$ with $p<q$ prime and $s=\sigma_1(N)-N-1=p+q$. Then
\[
  p \;=\; \frac{s-\sqrt{s^{2}-4N}}{2},
\]
with the square root exact by Theorem~\ref{thm:disc}.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The radicand is $(q-p)^2$, so the expression is $\bigl((p+q)-(q-p)\bigr)/2=p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Evaluation, not inversion, is the cost]\label{rem:evalinv}
The entire post-oracle cost of the $\sigma_1$-route is a constant number of
arithmetic operations. Whatever exponent Stratum~A carries is therefore an
exponent of \emph{evaluating} the arithmetic function, not of inverting it.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Evaluation costs $\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$, and the bound is attained}

Write $\scan(N)=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$ for the cost of evaluating $\tau$ or
$\sigma_1$ by the blind scan $d=1,\dots,\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$.

\begin{theorem}[The scan window is correct and tight]\label{thm:window}
For $N=pq$ with $p<q$ prime:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $p\le\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor<q$, so the scan sees exactly the divisors $1$
and $p$---half the divisor set;
\item the smaller factor is found at step $p\le\scan(N)$;
\item on a twin-prime semiprime $N=p(p+2)$ with $p>1$, $\scan(N)=p$ exactly, so
the worst case is attained.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) $p\le q$ gives $p^2\le N<q^2$. (3) $p^2\le p(p+2)<(p+1)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The exponent $1/2$, as a sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
For every $N\ge1$,
\[
  2\log\bigl(\scan(N)\bigr)\;\le\;\log N\;\le\;2\log\bigl(\scan(N)+1\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor^{2}\le N<(\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor+1)^{2}$; take logarithms.
\end{proof}

Hence $\log(\scan(N))/\log N\to1/2$. In continuous form the Stratum~A profile is
$S(x)=e^{x/2}$, which satisfies $S(\log N)=\sqrt N$ and sandwiches the discrete
scan: $\scan(N)\le\sqrt N<\scan(N)+1$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:expcal},
$\alpha(S)=1/2$.

\subsection{A hitting-set lower bound for structure-blindness}

\begin{definition}
A finite $S\subset\NN$ is a \emph{complete divisor-test set up to $B$} if for
every semiprime $N=pq$ with $p<q\le B$ some $s\in S$ properly splits $N$
($s\mid N$, $1<s<N$).
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Nothing else to find]\label{lem:nothing}
If $s$ properly splits $pq$ for distinct primes $p,q$, then $s\in\{p,q\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:divisors} the only divisors besides $1$ and $pq$ are $p,q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[At most one omission]\label{lem:omission}
A complete divisor-test set up to $B$ omits at most one prime $\le B$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If two distinct primes $p<q\le B$ were both absent, $pq$ would be split by no
element of $S$, contradicting completeness via Lemma~\ref{lem:nothing}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hitting-set bound]\label{thm:hitting}
If $S$ is a complete divisor-test set up to $B$, then $|S|\ge\pi(B)-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[No universal finite list]
For every finite $S$ there is a bound $B$ at which $S$ is not complete.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Linear growth in the bit-size]\label{thm:bertrand}
A complete divisor-test set up to $2^{k}$ has at least $k-1$ elements.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Bertrand's postulate supplies a prime in each $(2^{i},2^{i+1}]$ for
$0\le i<k$, so $\pi(2^{k})\ge k$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:hitting}.
\end{proof}

With $B\approx\sqrt N$, a procedure whose only access to $N$ is divisibility
testing against a fixed candidate list must carry essentially every prime below
$\sqrt N$. Pollard $\rho$ carries none.

\section{Stratum B: the classical methods, as theorems}

\subsection{Trial division}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:trial}
For primes $p\le q$, the least nontrivial divisor of $pq$ is $p$.
\end{theorem}

The certificate is the factor itself; the cost is at most $\lfloor\sqrt
N\rfloor$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:window}.

\subsection{Fermat: a dichotomy, and no early stop}

\begin{theorem}[Representation from a factorization]
If $2a=p+q$ and $2b=q-p$ then $a^{2}=pq+b^{2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Fermat dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $N=pq$ with $p,q$ prime and suppose $a^{2}=N+b^{2}$ with $a,b\ge0$. Then
either $\{a-b,a+b\}=\{1,N\}$ or $\{a-b,a+b\}=\{p,q\}$, i.e.\ $2a=p+q$ and
$2b=|q-p|$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(a-b)(a+b)=N$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:divisors} the only factorizations of a
semiprime are $1\cdot N$ and $p\cdot q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No early stop]\label{cor:noearly}
For $p<q$ the ascending search $a=\lceil\sqrt N\rceil,\lceil\sqrt
N\rceil+1,\dots$ halts for the first time exactly at $a=(p+q)/2$, so its cost is
the exact arithmetic quantity
$\fermatcost(N)=\frac{p+q}{2}-\lceil\sqrt N\rceil$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Complementarity]\label{thm:complement}
Neither Fermat nor trial division dominates the other:
\begin{enumerate}
\item on a twin-prime semiprime $N=p(p+2)$, Fermat halts at its first trial
$a=p+1$, while trial division needs $p=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$ steps;
\item on $N=3q$ with $q\ge48$, Fermat needs at least $q/4$ steps, while trial
division needs $2$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) $(p+1)^2=p(p+2)+1$. (2) The halting index is
$(3+q)/2-\lceil\sqrt{3q}\rceil>q/4$ once $\sqrt{3q}\le q/4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why the two look alike in aggregate]\label{rem:aggregate}
With $p=N^{\beta}$, $q=N^{1-\beta}$, $\beta\le1/2$, the trial cost is
$N^{\beta}$ and the Fermat cost is $\Theta(N^{1-2\beta})$: maxima at opposite
ends of the balance parameter, crossing at $\beta=1/3$. On uniform draws both
distributions are tail-dominated and their aggregate statistics coincide---a
fact about the draw measure, not about the methods.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Pollard $\rho$: exact extraction plus forced collision}

\begin{theorem}[Extraction]\label{thm:extract}
Let $N=pq$ with $p<q$ prime and $d\in\ZZ$ with $p\mid d$ and $N\nmid d$. Then
$\gcd(d,N)=p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\gcd(d,N)\in\{1,p,q,N\}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:divisors}; it is divisible by $p$,
excluding $1$ and $q$, and is not $N$ since $N\nmid d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Forced collision]\label{thm:pigeon}
For any $p>0$ and \emph{any} sequence $x:\NN\to\ZZ/p\ZZ$ there are $i<j\le p$
with $x_i=x_j$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Pigeonhole: $p+1$ terms into $p$ classes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Deterministic skeleton of Pollard $\rho$]\label{thm:rhoskel}
Within $p$ steps of any iteration some difference of iterates is divisible by
$p$; if it is not divisible by $N$, one gcd recovers $p$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

The birthday heuristic---a pseudorandom iteration collides modulo $p$ after
$\Theta(\sqrt p)$ steps---supplies the running time $\Theta(N^{1/4})$ for a
balanced semiprime. We record the profile $R(x)=e^{x/4}$, so
$\alpha(R)=1/4$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:expcal}.

\section{Stratum C: the quantum corner}

Shor's algorithm is quantum in exactly one step: computing the multiplicative
order $r$ of a unit modulo $N$. The reduction from $r$ to a factor is classical,
unconditional and exact.

\begin{theorem}[Split step]\label{thm:split}
Let $N=pq$ with $p,q$ distinct primes and $x\in\ZZ$ with $N\mid x^{2}-1$,
$N\nmid x-1$, $N\nmid x+1$. Then $\gcd(x-1,N)\in\{p,q\}$---never $1$ and never
$N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$N\mid(x-1)(x+1)$, so $p$ and $q$ each divide one factor; they cannot both
divide $x-1$, nor both $x+1$. Hence exactly one divides $x-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Shor's classical reduction]
If $a$ has order $2m$ modulo $N=pq$ and $a^{m}\not\equiv\pm1\pmod N$, then
$\gcd(a^{m}-1,N)\in\{p,q\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Nontriviality]
The factor produced is strictly between $1$ and $N$.
\end{theorem}

The quantum cost is polynomial in the bit-size; we record $Q(x)=8x^{3}$, so
$\alpha(Q)=0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:polycal}, and $Q$ is polynomially bounded.

\section{The plane}

\subsection{The three coordinates}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Stratum & Representative & Profile & Exponent\\
\midrule
A --- definition-routes & $\tau,\sigma_1$ by scan & $S(x)=e^{x/2}$ & $1/2$\\
B --- classical methods & Pollard $\rho$ & $R(x)=e^{x/4}$ & $1/4$\\
C --- quantum & Shor & $Q(x)=8x^{3}$ & $0$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{theorem}[The three coordinates]\label{thm:coords}
$\alpha(S)=1/2$, $\alpha(R)=1/4$, $\alpha(Q)=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Distinctness]
$S$ does not have exponent $1/4$, and $R$ does not have exponent $0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorems~\ref{thm:coords} and~\ref{thm:unique}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Strict ordering}

\begin{theorem}[Non-degeneracy of the plane]\label{thm:order}
For all $x>0$, $R(x)<S(x)$; and for all sufficiently large $x$, $Q(x)<R(x)$.
Hence eventually $Q(x)<R(x)<S(x)$ strictly.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first is $x/4<x/2$ and monotonicity of $\exp$. The second follows from
Theorem~\ref{thm:price} applied to $\alpha(Q)=0<1/4=\alpha(R)$: the ratio $R/Q$
diverges, so eventually exceeds $1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The tractability line}

\begin{theorem}[Quantum separation]\label{thm:quantumsep}
$Q$ is polynomially bounded; $R$ and $S$ are not.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$Q$ is a polynomial. For $R,S$, apply Corollary~\ref{cor:superpoly} with
exponents $1/4>0$ and $1/2>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A definition-route evaluated from $N$ alone cannot be a polynomial-time
factoring algorithm. This is unconditional; no hardness hypothesis is used.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The units ledger}\label{sec:units}

Let $\rho_{\mathrm{bit}}(b)=e^{b/2-\log 2}$ be the standalone calibration of
Pollard $\rho$ in the prime bit-size $b=\log p$: in base-two units,
$\log_2(\text{ops})=\text{bits}/2-1$ exactly.

\begin{theorem}
$\alpha(\rho_{\mathrm{bit}})=1/2$ in the variable $b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Units correction]\label{thm:unitscorr}
For a balanced semiprime $\log N=2\log p$, so the same cost re-expressed on $N$
is $x\mapsto\rho_{\mathrm{bit}}(x/2)$, and
$\alpha\bigl(\rho_{\mathrm{bit}}(\cdot/2)\bigr)=1/4$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:units} with $s=1/2$, $c=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The mismatch is a theorem]\label{thm:mismatch}
The re-expressed profile does \emph{not} have exponent $1/2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
It has exponent $1/4$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:unitscorr}, exponents are unique
(Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}), and $1/4\neq1/2$.
\end{proof}

A per-prime-bit slope is a legitimate measurement; reading it as an exponent on
$N$ is a unit error of a factor of two. Because the exponent is unique, that
error is a provable contradiction rather than a difference of convention: the
framework detects its own misuse.

\subsection{The price of structure-blindness}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{blindness price} is $P(x)=S(x)/R(x)$: the cost ratio between the
structure-blind definition-route and the structure-exploiting method at the same
$N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact price]\label{thm:priceexact}
$P(x)=e^{x/4}$; equivalently $P=N^{1/4}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The price is not a constant overhead]\label{thm:pricegrow}
$P$ is strictly increasing, has measured exponent $1/4$, and $P(x)\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Monotonicity from Theorem~\ref{thm:priceexact}; the exponent and divergence from
Theorem~\ref{thm:price} with $1/2-1/4=1/4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The three-strata plane]\label{thm:capstone}
Simultaneously: $\alpha(Q)=0$, $\alpha(R)=1/4$, $\alpha(S)=1/2$; eventually
$Q<R<S$ pointwise and strictly; and $P=S/R$ is strictly increasing with
$P(x)\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[A unit exchange rate]\label{rem:exchange}
$\alpha(P)=1/4=\alpha(R)$: the price of being blind is exactly the cost of the
method one declined to use. Ignorance and work trade one-for-one at every size.
\end{remark}

\section{Empirical calibration}

The theorems fix the exponents; the measurements below check finite-size
behaviour under identical conditions (fixed seeds, uniform semiprime draws, one
timing harness).

\paragraph{Stratum A.} Evaluating $\tau(N)$ and $\sigma_1(N)$ by trial
division---with $\sigma_1=1+N+p+q$ verified exactly at every size---yields a
fitted exponent of $0.500$ to three decimals, the numerical realisation of the
sandwich of Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}. Other definition-routes measured under
the same harness: gcd-scan $1.000$; idempotent scan $1.000$; zero-divisor first
hit $\approx1/2$; continued-fraction period $0.398$.

