\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{What the plane buys.} \emph{Comparability}: an arithmetic identity, a
randomised algorithm and a quantum subroutine are placed at $1/2$, $1/4$ and $0$
on one axis, measured the same way. \emph{Falsifiability}: uniqueness of the
exponent turns a units error into a contradiction
(Theorem~\ref{thm:mismatch}); in the measurement campaign the per-prime-bit
slope was reported first, flagged, corrected, and confirmed against an
independent calibration before entering the plane. \emph{Quantification}: the
price theorem upgrades ``much faster'' into the exact divergent factor
$N^{1/4}$.

\paragraph{Evaluation versus inversion.} The sharpest structural finding is
Remark~\ref{rem:evalinv}: for the $\sigma_1$-route the inversion is a closed
formula costing $O(1)$, so all of the $1/2$ is spent \emph{evaluating} the
arithmetic function from $N$ alone. Improving a definition-route means finding a
cheaper evaluation, never a cleverer inversion.

\paragraph{Aggregate statistics hide structure.} Fermat and trial division have
coincident mean and median on uniform draws yet are complementary at every
instance. A methodology comparing methods by summary statistics alone would
report them as equivalent; the dichotomy theorem, which makes the Fermat
stopping index exact, is what lets the difference be stated.

\paragraph{Structure-blindness has two faces.} An asymptotic one, the price
$N^{1/4}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:priceexact}), and a combinatorial one, the
hitting-set bound $|S|\ge\pi(B)-1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:hitting}). The second is
more robust: it constrains every procedure whose access to $N$ is divisibility
testing against a fixed candidate list.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The coordinate is deliberately coarse. It cannot
distinguish $N^{1/4}$ from $N^{1/4}\log N$, and it collapses the subexponential
family
$L_N[1/3,c]=\exp\bigl((c+o(1))(\log N)^{1/3}(\log\log N)^{2/3}\bigr)$---which
contains the number field sieve---to $\alpha=0$, alongside the genuinely
polynomial quantum profile. Refining the coordinate to resolve the $\alpha=0$
stratum is the most important open direction. The empirical figures are
finite-size and draw-distribution dependent; the theorems are not.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{1. The evaluation--inversion exponent gap.} Every definition-route
splits into an evaluation cost and an inversion cost; for $\sigma_1$ the
inversion is $O(1)$ while evaluation is $N^{1/2}$. Conjecture: the split is
universal across multiplicative witnesses and the plane's coordinate always
measures evaluation. The $O(1)$ half is proved
(Theorem~\ref{thm:closedform}); the general statement now has the vocabulary it
needs.

\paragraph{2. A balance-parameter law unifying Fermat and trial division.} With
$p=N^{\beta}$, $q=N^{1-\beta}$, the trial cost is $N^{\beta}$ and the Fermat
cost $\Theta(N^{1-2\beta})$, crossing at $\beta=1/3$: the plane should be drawn
in the $(\beta,\alpha)$ square, not on a line. The exact Fermat stop
$2a=p+q$ makes the cost an exact arithmetic expression rather than an empirical
count, so the crossing point is a theorem waiting to be stated.

\paragraph{3. The blindness price as a fixed exchange rate.} The price $N^{1/4}$
is not merely unbounded: it has the same exponent as the whole of Stratum~B
(Remark~\ref{rem:exchange}), so being blind and running $\rho$ cost the same at
every size. Extending the ledger to the smoothness barrier $L_N[1/3,c]$ would
produce the first non-exponential entry on the plane.

\section{Conclusion}

One coordinate, three strata, and every price measured rather than asserted.
Definition-routes sit at $\alpha=1/2$, with an exact closed-form inversion
showing the exponent belongs entirely to evaluation, and a hitting-set bound
showing no candidate list can be shorter. Classical methods sit at
$\alpha=1/4$, each given an exact statement rather than a citation, with
Fermat's apparent similarity to trial division exposed as an artefact of the
draw measure. The quantum corner sits at $\alpha=0$, its classical split step
unconditionally exact. The three are strictly ordered, provably distinct, and
separated across the tractability line. Between the top two floors stands the
price of structure-blindness, $N^{1/4}$: strictly increasing, unbounded, and
carrying the same exponent as the method it replaces---a unit exchange rate
between not knowing and working.

